::STAT_U01_L04_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best compares counts across favorite snack categories?
{
~A. Histogram#Histograms group numerical values.
~B. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show two numerical variables.
=C. Bar chart#A bar chart compares category counts.
~D. Box plot#Box plots summarize numerical data.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display lists categories and counts in rows?
{
=A. Frequency table#A frequency table lists each category with its count.
~B. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show numerical pairs.
~C. Histogram#Histograms group numerical intervals.
~D. Box plot#Box plots summarize numerical distributions.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is appropriate for two categorical variables such as grade level and club choice?
{
~A. Dot plot#Dot plots show one numerical variable.
=B. Two-way table#A two-way table organizes counts for combinations of categories.
~C. Histogram#Histograms display numerical intervals.
~D. Scatterplot#Scatterplots require two numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display could show each category as a part of a whole?
{
~A. Box plot#Box plots summarize numerical data.
~B. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show numerical relationships.
~C. Histogram#Histograms show numerical distributions.
=D. Pie chart#A pie chart can show proportions for one categorical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Why is a histogram not appropriate for favorite color?
{
~A. Favorite color cannot be counted#Responses can be counted by category.
=B. Favorite color is categorical, not numerical#Histograms need numerical intervals.
~C. Histograms are only for two variables#Histograms are for one numerical variable.
~D. Colors always require scatterplots#Scatterplots require numerical pairs.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which variable is categorical?
{
~A. Minutes spent commuting#Minutes are numerical.
~B. Distance traveled in miles#Miles are numerical.
~C. Number of bus stops#This is a numerical count.
=D. Transportation method#Transportation method uses group labels such as bus, walk, or car.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A relative frequency table is useful because it shows what?
{
~A. Exact paired numerical points#That is a scatterplot purpose.
=B. Proportions or percentages for categories#Relative frequency compares category shares.
~C. Quartiles and median#That is a box plot purpose.
~D. Only raw numerical intervals#That is a histogram purpose.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display would be weak for category names only?
{
~A. Bar chart#Bar charts compare category counts.
~B. Frequency table#Frequency tables list categories and counts.
~C. Pie chart#Pie charts can show category proportions.
=D. Scatterplot#Scatterplots require two numerical variables, not category names.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which question produces categorical data appropriate for a bar chart?
{
~A. How many minutes are spent on apps?#Minutes are numerical.
=B. Which app category is used most often?#App category is categorical and can be counted.
~C. What is the phone battery percentage?#Percentage is numerical.
~D. How many apps are installed?#Number of apps is numerical.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
When group sizes differ, what is often better than comparing counts only?
{
=A. Comparing relative frequencies#Relative frequencies make comparisons fairer when totals differ.
~B. Ignoring the group totals#Group totals affect count comparisons.
~C. Using a scatterplot for categories#Scatterplots do not fit two categorical variables.
~D. Changing category names into averages#Category names cannot be averaged.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display choice matches one categorical variable?
{
~A. Box plot#Box plots require numerical data.
~B. Histogram#Histograms require numerical intervals.
=C. Bar chart#A bar chart can show counts for one categorical variable.
~D. Scatterplot#Scatterplots require two numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A two-way table is best used when data include what?
{
=A. Two categorical variables#Two-way tables summarize combinations of two categorical variables.
~B. One numerical variable only#A dot plot or histogram would fit better.
~C. Two numerical variables#A scatterplot would fit better.
~D. A five-number summary#A box plot displays that summary.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which statement about pie charts is accurate?
{
=A. They show how categories divide a whole#Each slice represents a category proportion.
~B. They show paired numerical relationships#That is a scatterplot.
~C. They show numerical intervals#That is a histogram.
~D. They show median and quartiles#That is a box plot.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which categorical display can compare counts with rectangular bars?
{
~A. Box plot#Box plots summarize numerical data.
~B. Histogram#Histogram bars represent numerical intervals, not category labels.
~C. Scatterplot#Scatterplots use points, not category bars.
=D. Bar chart#Bar lengths or heights compare category counts.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which is the best reason to label categories clearly?
{
~A. Labels turn categories into numerical values#Labels name groups; they do not make quantities.
~B. Labels replace the need for counts#Counts or percentages are still needed.
=C. Labels tell what each group represents#Clear labels prevent misinterpretation of category counts.
~D. Labels prove cause and effect#Labels do not establish causes.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A table shows favorite subject by grade level. What display type fits this structure?
{
~A. Histogram#A histogram requires one numerical variable.
~B. Box plot#A box plot summarizes numerical values.
=C. Two-way table#Both variables are categorical.
~D. Scatterplot#A scatterplot requires two numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is not designed for categorical data?
{
~A. Frequency table#Frequency tables work for categorical data.
~B. Bar chart#Bar charts work for categorical data.
~C. Pie chart#Pie charts can show categorical proportions.
=D. Box plot#A box plot summarizes numerical data using quartiles.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Why might a percentage display be better than raw counts?
{
=A. Percentages account for different group totals#Percentages support fair comparisons across unequal groups.
~B. Percentages remove the need for categories#Categories are still being compared.
~C. Percentages turn data into scatterplots#Scatterplots use numerical pairs, not category percentages alone.
~D. Percentages always prove causation#Percentages summarize data but do not prove causes.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which context fits a frequency table?
{
~A. Temperature each hour#Temperature is numerical.
=B. Counts of students by preferred club#A frequency table lists each club category and count.
~C. Height and weight for each student#That is bivariate numerical.
~D. Quiz score distribution in intervals#That is a histogram context.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display could summarize yes/no responses by grade level?
{
~A. Dot plot#Dot plots show numerical values.
~B. Box plot#Box plots summarize numerical distributions.
~C. Histogram#Histograms show numerical intervals.
=D. Two-way table#Grade level and yes/no response are categorical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
What does the height of a bar represent in a category bar chart?
{
~A. The category name length#Name length does not determine bar height.
~B. The median of the category#Categories do not have medians.
=C. Count or frequency for that category#Bar height shows how many responses fall in the category.
~D. A paired numerical value#Category bars summarize counts, not pairs.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which is a categorical variable?
{
=A. Favorite streaming service#Service names are category labels.
~B. Hours streamed per week#Hours are numerical.
~C. Monthly subscription cost#Cost is numerical.
~D. Number of shows watched#Number of shows is a count.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is best for showing percentages in categories out of 100 percent?
{
~A. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show numerical pairs.
=B. Pie chart#A pie chart shows category shares of a whole.
~C. Histogram#Histograms show numerical intervals.
~D. Box plot#Box plots summarize numerical distributions.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A student uses a bar chart for test scores 0 through 100 grouped by intervals. What would be more appropriate?
{
=A. Histogram#Grouped numerical intervals are represented with a histogram.
~B. Pie chart#Pie charts are for categorical parts of a whole.
~C. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables.
~D. Scatterplot#A scatterplot is for two numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L04_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L04_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which justification best supports a bar chart for favorite sport?
{
~A. Favorite sport is numerical, and bars show intervals#Sport names are not numerical intervals.
~B. Favorite sport has two numerical variables#Only one categorical variable is described.
=C. Favorite sport is categorical, and bars compare counts by category#The display matches categorical data and the comparison goal.
~D. Favorite sport requires quartiles#Quartiles apply to numerical data, not sport categories.
}
